https://www.maths.cam.ac.uk/undergrad/pastpapers/files/2005/PaperII_3.pdf

= 1H
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Number Theory}

= Solution
{parent=1H}

Let $p_1,\ldots,p_a$ be the first $a$ <primes>, and let $\phi(x,a)$ count positive integers at most $x$ not divisible by any of them. The <inclusion-exclusion principle> gives
$$
\phi(x,a)=\sum_{S\subseteq\{1,\ldots,a\}}(-1)^{|S|}\left\lfloor\frac{x}{\prod_{j\in S}p_j}\right\rfloor.
$$
For $a=\pi(\sqrt x)$, every composite at most $x$ is removed, so the survivors are one and the <primes> larger than $\sqrt x$. Thus the <Legendre prime-counting formula> is $\boxed{\pi(x)=\phi(x,\pi(\sqrt x))+\pi(\sqrt x)-1}$. This is the prime-counting formula, distinct from the factorial-valuation formula of the same name.

Fix $y\geq2$ and put $a=\pi(y)$. Every <prime> exceeding $y$ is among the unsieved integers. Replacing each floor in the finite sum by its argument makes an error bounded by $2^a$, giving
$$
\pi(x)\leq a+\phi(x,a)\leq a+x\prod_{p\leq y}(1-1/p)+2^a.
$$
Take $x\to\infty$ with $y$ fixed. The supplied product estimate gives $0\leq\limsup\pi(x)/x\leq1/\log y$. Since $y$ can be arbitrarily large, $\boxed{\lim_{x\to\infty}\pi(x)/x=0}$. The order of these limits avoids treating the growing inclusion-exclusion error as negligible.

= 2F
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Topics in Analysis}

= i
{parent=2f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For each node define its <Lagrange interpolation> cardinal <polynomial> $\ell_i(t)=\prod_{j\ne i}(t-x_j)/(x_i-x_j)$. Its square has degree $2n-2<2n$ and nodal values $\ell_i(x_j)^2=\delta_{ij}$. Exactness therefore gives $a_i=\int_{-1}^1\ell_i(t)^2dt$. The <polynomial> is nonzero, so continuity makes this integral strictly positive. Thus $\boxed{a_i>0}$ for every node.

= ii
{parent=2f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Apply exactness to the constant <polynomial> one. Its integral is two and its value at every node is one, so $\boxed{\sum_{i=1}^n a_i=2}$.

= iii
{parent=2f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Put $P(t)=\prod_i(t-x_i)$. For every <polynomial> $q$ of degree at most $n-1$, $Pq$ has degree at most $2n-1$ and vanishes at all nodes; thus $\int_{-1}^1Pq=0$. The monic degree-$n$ <orthogonal polynomial> for this integral is unique: the difference of two such monic <polynomials> has degree below $n$ and is orthogonal to itself, hence is zero. Consequently $P$ is the monic normalization of the degree-$n$ <Legendre polynomial>, proving **the given nodes are exactly its roots**. Together the three results characterize this <Gaussian quadrature> rule.

= 3G
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Geometry of Group Actions}

= Solution
{parent=3G}

A nonidentity <Möbius transformation> has either two distinct fixed points on the Riemann sphere or one double fixed point. With two fixed points, conjugate them to zero and infinity. A Möbius map fixing both is $z\mapsto kz$ with $k\ne0$. With one fixed point, conjugate it to infinity; the resulting map is affine, $z\mapsto az+b$. If $a\ne1$ it would have another fixed point, so $a=1$ and $b\ne0$. Scaling the coordinate makes it $z\mapsto z+1$. The identity is already the case $k=1$.

Define $\operatorname{tr}^2(g)$ using a determinant-one representing <matrix>; the two possible lifts differ by sign, so its square is well defined and invariant under conjugation. For $z\mapsto kz$ it is $k+2+k^{-1}$, while for a nontrivial translation it is four. If two nonidentity dilations have equal trace-square, their multipliers satisfy the same quadratic and are either equal or reciprocal. Inversion $z\mapsto1/z$ conjugates one reciprocal multiplier to the other. Trace-square four forces the dilation multiplier to be one, which is the excluded identity; thus every nonidentity map with that invariant is in the translation class. This proves $\boxed{g\sim h\iff\operatorname{tr}^2(g)=\operatorname{tr}^2(h)}$ for the stated nonidentity maps.

**Not every such map fixes an interior point of hyperbolic three-space.** In its upper-half-space extension, $z\mapsto z+1$ translates the horizontal coordinate and has no fixed point at positive height. A dilation extends as $(z,t)\mapsto(kz,|k|t)$, so it fixes the vertical axis only when $|k|=1$; if $|k|\ne1$, the height changes. Boundary fixed points therefore do not imply an interior fixed point.

= 4J
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Coding and Cryptography}

= Solution
{parent=4J}

A <digital signature> lets a holder of a private key authenticate a message while anyone with the public key verifies it. Signing a message digest ties the signature to the message and detects alterations; it does not conceal the message. Authentication also requires a trustworthy association of the public key with its owner.

For the <ElGamal signature scheme>, choose a large <prime> $p$ and a generator $g$ of $\mathbb F_p^\times$. The secret is $x$, and the public key is $(p,g,y)$ with $y=g^x\bmod p$. To sign a message whose hash is represented by $m\bmod(p-1)$, choose a fresh secret nonce $k$ coprime to $p-1$ and set
$$
\boxed{r=g^k\bmod p,\qquad s=k^{-1}(m-xr)\bmod(p-1).}
$$
Transmit $(r,s)$ with the message. Verification checks the permitted ranges and $\boxed{g^m\equiv y^r r^s\pmod p}$. Correctness follows from $xr+ks\equiv m\pmod{p-1}$. The secret exponent prevents an outsider from directly constructing this relation for an arbitrary hashed message; nonce reuse can expose the exponent through the repeated linear congruences. Hashing is part of the signature scheme, rather than signing arbitrary raw exponents with no message encoding.

= 5I
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Statistical Modelling}

= a
{parent=5i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <least-squares estimator> minimizes $\|Y-Xb\|^2$. Differentiating in $b$ gives $-2X^T(Y-Xb)=0$, hence $\boxed{X^TX\widehat\beta=X^TY}$. Full column rank makes $X^TX$ positive definite, so
$$
\widehat\beta=(X^TX)^{-1}X^TY=\beta+(X^TX)^{-1}X^T\epsilon.
$$
This is a <linear transformation> of a <multivariate normal distribution>. Its mean is $\beta$ and its <covariance> is $\sigma^2(X^TX)^{-1}$. Therefore $\boxed{\widehat\beta\sim N_p(\beta,\sigma^2(X^TX)^{-1})}$.

= b
{parent=5i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Define the <hat matrix> $H=X(X^TX)^{-1}X^T$. It is symmetric and idempotent, the <orthogonal projection> onto the column space of $X$. Since $HX=X$,
$$
\widehat Y=HY=X\beta+H\epsilon,\qquad
\boxed{\widehat Y\sim N_n(X\beta,\sigma^2H).}
$$
The <covariance> is $\sigma^2HH^T=\sigma^2H$. This <normal distribution> can be singular when $p<n$, since all fitted vectors lie in the $p$-dimensional model subspace.

= 6E
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Mathematical Biology}

= a
{parent=6e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The probability of an exact copy is $Q=q^L$. Incorrect copies contribute mutants at rate $a(1-Q)x$, giving $\boxed{\dot y=by+a(1-Q)x}$. For $y>0$, differentiating the ratio gives
$$
\boxed{\dot z=(aQ-b)z-a(1-Q)z^2.}
$$
This is a logistic-type equation for relative concentration; the absolute concentrations need not have a stationary value.

= b
{parent=6e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For $0<q<1$, a finite positive equilibrium exists exactly when $a q^L>b$, and then
$$
\boxed{z_*=(a q^L-b)/[a(1-q^L)].}
$$
The derivative of the ratio's <vector field> at $z_*$ is $-(aq^L-b)<0$, so this equilibrium is locally asymptotically stable; every positive initial ratio tends to it. The critical real length is $L_c=\log(b/a)/\log q$, and the admissible integer lengths satisfy $L<L_c$. Thus the largest positive integer length, if one exists, is $\boxed{L_{\max}=\lceil L_c\rceil-1}$. At equality the equilibrium is zero, so rounding down without accounting for the strict inequality can be wrong. If $q=1$, mutants are never produced from masters and the ratio grows without a finite positive equilibrium; the finite threshold formula concerns imperfect copying.

= c
{parent=6e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Write $\epsilon=1-q$ and $b/a=1-s$. Then $\log(1-s)=-s+O(s^2)$ and $\log q=-\epsilon+O(\epsilon^2)$, giving
$$
\boxed{L_c\simeq\frac{s}{1-q}.}
$$
The largest permitted integer is still selected by the strict inequality from part (b). This says that longer master sequences need a proportionately larger selective advantage or smaller per-digit copying error.

= 7B
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Dynamical Systems}

= Solution
{parent=7B}

At a hyperbolic saddle, the stable invariant subspace of $Df(0)$ is the sum of its <generalized eigenspaces> with negative real parts; the unstable subspace uses positive real parts. The <stable manifold theorem> gives invariant local manifolds tangent to these subspaces at the equilibrium, with the corresponding dimensions and the appropriate forward or backward convergence to the equilibrium.

Here the Jacobian is $\operatorname{diag}(1,-1)$, so the unstable tangent is the $x$ axis and the stable tangent the $y$ axis. For the stable graph write $x=h(y)=Ay^2+By^3+O(y^4)$. Invariance requires $h'(y)(-y+3h(y)^2)=h+h^2+2hy+3y^2$. Comparing quadratic and cubic terms gives $-2A=A+3$ and $-3B=B+2A$, so $A=-1,B=1/2$.

For the unstable graph $y=g(x)=Cx^2+Dx^3+O(x^4)$, invariance is $g'(x)(x+x^2+2xg+3g^2)=-g+3x^2$. Coefficients give $2C=3-C$ and $2C+3D=-D$, so $C=1,D=-1/2$. Thus the <cubic graph expansion of a saddle invariant manifold> is
$$
\boxed{W^s:\ x=-y^2+y^3/2+O(y^4),\qquad W^u:\ y=x^2-x^3/2+O(x^4).}
$$
These describe the local manifolds to cubic order, not exact global <polynomial> curves.

= 8A
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Further Complex Methods}

= Solution
{parent=8A}

Assume the Laplace integrals converge absolutely in a common right half-plane. The <Fubini theorem> and the substitution $t=u-v$ on $u\geq v\geq0$ give the <convolution theorem for Laplace transforms>:
$$
\begin{aligned}
\widehat h(p)&=\int_0^\infty\int_0^u e^{-pu}f(u-v)g(v)\,dv\,du\\
&=\int_0^\infty\int_0^\infty e^{-p(t+v)}f(t)g(v)\,dt\,dv
=\boxed{\widehat f(p)\widehat g(p)}.
\end{aligned}
$$
For the specified inverse-square-root function, the supplied identity says $f*f=\pi$ on $t>0$. Consequently $\widehat f(p)^2=\pi/p$. For real $p>0$ the defining integral is positive, fixing the positive square root; analytic continuation into $\operatorname{Re}p>0$ gives
$$
\boxed{\widehat f(p)=\sqrt\pi\,p^{-1/2},}
$$
with the branch positive on the positive real axis. The square identity alone would not select the sign.

= 9C
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Classical Dynamics}

= Solution
{parent=9C}

The <Poisson bracket> is $\{f,g\}=\sum_a(f_{q_a}g_{p_a}-f_{p_a}g_{q_a})$. For a function with no explicit time dependence, the chain rule and <Hamilton equations> give
$$
\frac{df}{dt}=\sum_a(f_{q_a}\dot q_a+f_{p_a}\dot p_a)=\sum_a(f_{q_a}H_{p_a}-f_{p_a}H_{q_a})=\boxed{\{f,H\}}.
$$
With $L_b=\epsilon_{bcd}x_cp_d$, direct differentiation gives
$$
\boxed{\{p_a,L_b\}=-\epsilon_{bad}p_d=\epsilon_{abd}p_d,\qquad \{x_a,L_b\}=\epsilon_{abc}x_c.}
$$
Apply the product rule to $L_a=\epsilon_{acd}x_cp_d$ and substitute these two brackets. Contracting the epsilon symbols yields $\boxed{\{L_a,L_b\}=\epsilon_{abc}L_c}$. Equivalently, the two product terms are precisely the infinitesimal rotation of the vector $x\times p$ about the $b$ axis, with the same bracket convention as the position and momentum vectors.

= 10D
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Cosmology}

= a
{parent=10d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For a homogeneous isotropic metric, radial light propagation covers comoving distance $\int c\,dt/a(t)$. The physical <particle horizon> is $d_p(t)=a(t)\int_{t_i}^t c\,dt'/a(t')$, when this is finite. A future <event horizon> exists when $d_e(t)=a(t)\int_t^{t_f}c\,dt'/a(t')$ is finite. The <Hubble radius> is the instantaneous scale $c/H$, with $H=\dot a/a$; it is not generally the same as either causal horizon.

For an <Einstein-de Sitter universe>, $H=2/(3t)$, so $c/H=3ct/2$, while $d_p=3ct$ from a beginning at zero. The future integral diverges, so there is no event horizon. For an eternal-past <de Sitter spacetime>, $H$ is constant and the particle-horizon integral from $-\infty$ diverges. Its event horizon and Hubble radius both equal $c/H$. Thus **a Hubble radius is not automatically a particle horizon**, even in these simple isotropic models.

= b
{parent=10d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For an expanding decelerating power law $a\propto t^\alpha$ with $0<\alpha<1$, the particle horizon is $ct/(1-\alpha)$, and the comoving Hubble radius is $c/(aH)\propto t^{1-\alpha}$. It increases with time, so regions that are mutually visible today can have been out of causal contact at early times. The nearly uniform early thermal conditions inferred across such regions give the <horizon problem>.

During <cosmic inflation>, accelerated expansion makes the comoving Hubble radius decrease. A patch initially within one causal communication scale can expand into the region now observed, after which its separated parts are temporarily outside the Hubble radius and later re-enter during deceleration. Sufficient duration before the hot radiation era permits a common causal origin; accelerated expansion is not itself a claim that signals propagate faster than light locally.

= c
{parent=10d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For the radiation-era law beginning at $t_r$, the present particle-horizon estimate is
$$
d_H(t_0)=2ct_0\left(1-\sqrt{t_r/t_0}\right)\simeq2ct_0\simeq6\times10^{25}\ \mathrm m.
$$
Rescale this present physical distance back to $t_r$ using $a(t_r)/a(t_0)=\sqrt{t_r/t_0}$. Its physical size was $2c\sqrt{t_rt_0}\simeq0.6\ \mathrm m$, of order one metre as required. The matched inflationary Hubble rate is $H=1/(2t_r)\simeq5\times10^{34}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}$, with Hubble radius $c/H\simeq6\times10^{-27}\ \mathrm m$.

If inflation produces $N$ e-folds before $t_r$, this patch began with size $0.6e^{-N}$ metres. It fits within one Hubble radius if
$$
\boxed{e^N\gtrsim10^{26},\qquad N\gtrsim26\log10\simeq60.}
$$
This estimate uses the idealized radiation-only history stated in the question; substituting the actual matter-dominated history would change the numerical estimate.

= 11H
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Number Theory}

= Solution
{parent=11H}

Write a reduced positive definite <binary quadratic form> as $[a,b,c]=ax^2+bxy+cy^2$, with $|b|\leq a\leq c$ and $b\geq0$ on the reduction boundaries. Since $4ac-b^2=20$, reduction gives $3a^2\leq20$, so $a=1$ or two. The middle coefficient is even. For $a=1$, only $b=0$ is possible, yielding $c=5$. For $a=2$, $b=0$ would give a noninteger $c$, while $b=\pm2$ gives $c=3$; the boundary convention selects $b=2$. Thus the two reduced forms are
$$
\boxed{x^2+5y^2,\qquad 2x^2+2xy+3y^2.}
$$

A positive odd integer $n$ is properly represented by some integer form of discriminant $d$ exactly when $b^2\equiv d\pmod{4n}$ has a solution. For a specified proper equivalence class, the more precise criterion is that $[n,b,(b^2-d)/(4n)]$ belong to that class for some such $b$. Indeed, a primitive representing vector extends to an $SL_2(\mathbb Z)$ basis change, making the leading coefficient $n$; conversely that form represents $n$ at the primitive vector $(1,0)$.

For <primes> $p\nmid10$, the existence criterion is $(-20/p)=1$, because the congruence modulo four is compatible with an even middle coefficient. Quadratic reciprocity gives $(-20/p)=(-1/p)(p/5)$, so the split possibilities are $p\equiv1,3,7,9\pmod{20}$. Any proper representing form reduces to one of the two listed forms. Odd values of $x^2+5y^2$ are one modulo four; odd values of $2x^2+2xy+3y^2$ require odd $y$ and are three modulo four. These distinguish the two classes. Consequently
$$
\boxed{p=x^2+5y^2\text{ for a prime }p\ne2,5\iff p\equiv1\text{ or }9\pmod{20}.}
$$
A <representation> of a <prime> is automatically proper: a common divisor of $x,y$ would have its square divide $p$.

= 12J
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Coding and Cryptography}

= Solution
{parent=12J}

A linear length-$n$ <cyclic code> is a subspace closed under cyclic coordinate shifts. Identify a word with its <polynomial> modulo $X^n-1$; multiplying by $X$ performs the shift. Thus a cyclic code is an <ideal> of $\mathbb F_q[X]/(X^n-1)$. Its preimage in the <polynomial> ring is an <ideal> containing $X^n-1$, so the <Euclidean algorithm> gives a unique monic generator $g$ dividing $X^n-1$. The <generator polynomial of a cyclic code> is $g$ and the <check polynomial of a cyclic code> is $h=(X^n-1)/g$; a word lies in the code exactly when $hc\equiv0\pmod{X^n-1}$.

For the binary length-seven code, the given $h$ factors with
$$
\boxed{g(X)=X^3+X+1,\qquad (X^3+X+1)(X^4+X^2+X+1)=X^7+1.}
$$
This cubic has no binary root and is irreducible. Let $\alpha$ be its root in $\mathbb F_8$. Since the nonzero multiplicative group has <prime> order seven and $\alpha\ne1$, its seven powers enumerate all nonzero elements. The condition $c(\alpha)=0$ gives a three-row binary parity-check <matrix> whose columns are precisely the seven nonzero vectors of $\mathbb F_2^3$. This is <Hamming's code> in a cyclic coordinate order. It has dimension four, and minimum distance three: no one or two distinct nonzero columns sum to zero, while $g$ itself has weight three. Its sixteen radius-one balls have total size $16(1+7)=128$, confirming the original perfect Hamming code.

It actually contains its <dual code> in this coordinate order. Each parity-check row has four ones, and any two distinct rows have two simultaneous ones, so $HH^T=0$ over $\mathbb F_2$. The row space $C^\perp$ therefore lies in $\ker H=C$. Equivalently the dual generator is the reciprocal check <polynomial>
$$
h^*(X)=X^4+X^3+X^2+1=(X+1)g(X),
$$
which is divisible by $g$. Hence **there is a three-dimensional subcode equal to the dual**, and in any other coordinate order it is equivalent to that dual.

= 13E
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Mathematical Biology}

= a
{parent=13e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Let $m_i=\langle x_i\rangle$. The mean equations give $m_1=\lambda_1/\beta_1$ and $m_2=\lambda_2m_1/\beta_2$. For relative deviations, the drift Jacobian and normalized reaction-noise <covariance> are
$$
A=\begin{pmatrix}-\beta_1&0\\\beta_2&-\beta_2\end{pmatrix},\qquad D=\operatorname{diag}(2\beta_1/m_1,2\beta_2/m_2).
$$
The <normalized stationary fluctuation-dissipation relation for a reaction network> is $A\eta+\eta A^T+D=0$ with this negative-drift convention. Its entries successively give
$$
\boxed{\eta_{11}=\frac1{m_1},\qquad \eta_{12}=\frac{\beta_2}{\beta_1+\beta_2}\frac1{m_1},\qquad
\eta_{22}=\frac1{m_2}+\frac{\beta_2}{\beta_1+\beta_2}\frac1{m_1}.}
$$
The birth and death channels act on one species at a time, so the diffusion <matrix> is diagonal even though the stationary <covariance> is not.

= b
{parent=13e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

If RNA concentration does not fluctuate, protein synthesis is an immigration-death process, so its intrinsic normalized <variance> is $\boxed{1/m_2}$. If protein birth/death noise is suppressed and protein responds deterministically to the fluctuating RNA input, the remaining normalized <variance> is
$$
\boxed{\eta_{22}^{\rm ext}=\frac{\beta_2}{\beta_1+\beta_2}\eta_{11}.}
$$
Writing $\tau_i=1/\beta_i$, its factor is $\tau_1/(\tau_1+\tau_2)$. Slow environmental changes are followed almost fully; rapid environmental fluctuations are averaged over the longer protein response time. Thus extrinsic noise is the magnitude of relative RNA fluctuations multiplied by a dynamical averaging factor.

= c
{parent=13e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

At the deterministic feedback equilibrium use $\lambda_2m_1f(m_2)=\beta_2m_2$. The indicated elasticity is
$$
H_{22}=1-\frac{\partial\log f}{\partial\log x_2}=1+h=:H.
$$
The normalized drift becomes $A=\begin{pmatrix}-\beta_1&0\\\beta_2&-\beta_2H\end{pmatrix}$; equal mean birth and death rates still give $D=\operatorname{diag}(2\beta_1/m_1,2\beta_2/m_2)$. Solving the same <covariance> equation yields the <feedback attenuation of gene-expression noise>:
$$
\boxed{\eta_{22}\simeq\frac1{Hm_2}+\frac{\beta_2}{H(\beta_1+\beta_2H)m_1}.}
$$
For $f(x_2)=k/x_2$, $h=1,H=2$, so
$$
\boxed{m_2\simeq\sqrt{\lambda_2km_1/\beta_2},\qquad \eta_{22}\simeq\frac1{2m_2}+\frac{\beta_2}{2(\beta_1+2\beta_2)m_1}.}
$$
The inverse-feedback expression is interpreted near a positive equilibrium; a microscopic birth rate singular at zero needs a separate regularization there.

= d
{parent=13e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Factor the extrinsic term as
$$
\boxed{\eta_{22}^{\rm ext}\simeq\eta_{11}\,\frac1{H^2}\,\frac{\beta_2H}{\beta_1+\beta_2H}.}
$$
The first factor is environmental relative <variance>. The second is static susceptibility squared: differentiating the equilibrium relation gives $\delta\log m_2=H^{-1}\delta\log m_1$. The last is the time-averaging factor with effective protein response time $1/(\beta_2H)$. Feedback makes response faster, which by itself weakens averaging, but simultaneously reduces the static gain. These are distinct effects and both must be retained.

= e
{parent=13e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

For the constitutive system, every propensity is affine in the copy numbers. The exact first- and second-moment equations therefore close: expectations of drift are determined by the means, and <covariance> drift by the <covariance>. The stationary fluctuation-dissipation calculation is consequently exact, even though the full joint stationary distribution need not be Gaussian.

For feedback, expectations such as $E[x_1f(x_2)]$ involve nonlinear moments and correlations. Replacing them by a linear expansion about the mean is a <linear noise approximation>, so the <covariance> formula is approximate. It works well around a stable single equilibrium when relative fluctuations are small and the rates vary smoothly over their typical range, generally at sufficiently large molecule numbers. Low counts, strong nonlinearities, multiple attractors and proximity to instability can invalidate it.

= f
{parent=13e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=f}

With response rates and elasticity effectively fixed, changing $\lambda_2$ changes $m_2$ and hence intrinsic noise but not the environmental term. Observation (i) therefore indicates **extrinsic noise dominates**, making the mean-dependent intrinsic contribution negligible. For a general feedback law whose elasticity changes substantially with the mean, compensation between terms is another possibility; independence from $\lambda_2$ alone would then not logically identify the dominant term.

Observation (ii) measures the local relaxation rate $\beta_2H$, which is larger under negative feedback. Observation (iii) is not explained by faster adjustment alone. At fixed susceptibility, increasing the response rate increases $\beta_2H/(\beta_1+\beta_2H)$, reducing time averaging and increasing transmitted environmental noise. Feedback lowers <variance> primarily by reducing susceptibility and the intrinsic term. Thus **rapid adjustment and low noise share a feedback mechanism but are not a direct causal equivalence**.

= 14A
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Further Complex Methods}

= Solution
{parent=14A}

Differentiate under the contour integral, with one consistent branch and convergent endpoint behavior. Then
$$
zw''+2kw'+zw=\int_\gamma\big[z(t^2+1)+2kt\big](t^2+1)^{k-1}e^{zt}dt
=\int_\gamma\frac{d}{dt}\big[(t^2+1)^ke^{zt}\big]dt.
$$
The stated zero boundary term therefore makes the integral a solution. On a closed contour this means returning to the same branch; on an open contour it requires the endpoint values to cancel or vanish.

= i
{parent=14a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For $\operatorname{Re}k>0$, choose the straight path from $-i$ to $i$ and put $t=is$, $-1\leq s\leq1$. Choose the power of $1-s^2$ continuously, positive for real positive $k$. The boundary factor vanishes at both endpoints. At zero,
$$
w(0)=i\int_{-1}^1(1-s^2)^{k-1}ds=i\int_0^1u^{-1/2}(1-u)^{k-1}du
=\boxed{iB(k,1/2)},
$$
using the symmetric <beta function> integral. This choice also gives a nonzero solution.

= ii
{parent=14a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For positive integers $n$, the finite open segment from part (i) works because $k=n/2>0$. For $n\leq0$, take a finite closed contour enclosing both <branch points> $i,-i$, with the integrand analytic in the exterior of the joining cut. Near infinity choose $(t^2+1)^{n/2-1}=t^{n-2}(1+t^{-2})^{n/2-1}$. Since $n$ is integral, a circuit around both <branch points> returns to the initial branch, and the endpoint boundary term cancels. Thus this is an admissible finite contour, including $n=0$.

For $n\leq-1$, at $z=0$ that exterior branch is $O(t^{n-2})=O(t^{-3})$. Deforming the contour to a large circle makes its integral tend to zero; equivalently its Laurent series has no $t^{-1}$ coefficient. Hence $\boxed{w(0)=0\quad(n\leq-1)}$. An individual loop around one <branch point> would not generally return to the same branch when $n$ is odd, which is why both are enclosed.

= 15C
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Classical Dynamics}

= i
{parent=15c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <action variable> for a regular periodic one-degree-of-freedom Hamiltonian orbit is $I(E)=(2\pi)^{-1}\oint p\,dq$, with orientation along the motion. Along its energy curve, implicit differentiation gives $\partial p/\partial E=1/H_p=1/\dot q$. Differentiating the loop integral branchwise gives
$$
\frac{dI}{dE}=\frac1{2\pi}\oint\frac{dq}{\dot q}=\frac1{2\pi}\oint dt,
\qquad \boxed{T=2\pi\frac{dI}{dE}.}
$$
At simple turning points the two momentum branches join and their endpoint contributions cancel, so the integrable turning-point singularities do not spoil this differentiation. The formula concerns regular periodic levels, not a separatrix of infinite period.

= ii
{parent=15c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The energy equation rearranges to $p^2+(E+1)q^2=E\mu^2$, an ellipse with semiaxes $p_0=\mu\sqrt E$ and $q_0=\mu\sqrt{E/(E+1)}$. At low energy it is nearly circular with both radii $\mu\sqrt E$; at high energy it is tall and thin, with $q_0\to\mu$ while $p_0$ grows. The area enclosed is $\pi p_0q_0$, so
$$
\boxed{I(E)=\frac{\mu^2E}{2\sqrt{E+1}},\qquad T(E)=\frac{\pi\mu^2(E+2)}{2(E+1)^{3/2}}.}
$$
Consequently $T\sim\pi\mu^2/(2\sqrt E)=\boxed{\pi\mu^3/(2p_0)}$ at high energy.

\Image[paper-3-orbits.png]
{title=Energy ellipses for the one-dimensional Hamiltonian at low and high energy, with mu equal to one}

= 16F
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Logic and Set Theory}

= Solution
{parent=16F}

The <axiom of foundation> states that every nonempty set $A$ has a member $a$ disjoint from $A$. The principle of membership induction states that a property $P$ satisfying $\forall x[(\forall y\in x\ P(y))\Rightarrow P(x)]$ holds for every set.

Assume Foundation and suppose a progressive property fails at a set $x$. In the transitive closure of $\{x\}$, form the nonempty set of counterexamples by Separation. Foundation selects a counterexample $a$ with no counterexample among its members. Transitivity makes those members belong to the closure, so each satisfies $P$, and progressiveness gives $P(a)$, a contradiction. Conversely, if a nonempty $A$ had no member disjoint from it, the property $P(x):x\notin A$ would be progressive: a member of $A$ necessarily has a member in $A$. Membership induction would then say no set belongs to $A$, a contradiction. Thus **Foundation and membership induction are equivalent**.

Apply induction to the property that a set belongs to some stage of the <cumulative hierarchy>. For each member $y$ of $x$, choose its least stage ordinal. Replacement collects these ordinals into a set; take an ordinal $\gamma$ large enough that all members lie in $V_\gamma$. Then $x\subseteq V_\gamma$ and $x\in V_{\gamma+1}$. This proves every set appears in the hierarchy.

For finite stages let $a_n=|V_n|$. Then $\boxed{a_0=0,\quad a_{n+1}=2^{a_n}}$, giving $0,1,2,4,16,65536,\ldots$. The stage $V_\omega$ is countably infinite, as the union of the finite stages. Thus $|V_{\omega+1}|=2^{\aleph_0}=|\mathbb R|$. Cantor's theorem makes $|V_{\omega+2}|$ strictly larger, and every later stage contains it. Earlier stages have at most countable size. Therefore $\boxed{|V_\alpha|=|\mathbb R|\iff\alpha=\omega+1}$.

= 17F
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Graph Theory}

= Solution
{parent=17F}

For fixed $k$-element subsets $U,V$, their rectangle is empty with probability $(1-p)^{k^2}$. A <union bound> over at most $n^{2k}$ choices gives $P(A)\leq n^{2k}(1-p)^{k^2}$. For a specified vertex and a specified $(n-k)$-subset, the chance of no edges is $(1-p)^{n-k}$. There are at most $n\binom nk\leq n^{k+1}$ choices on each side, giving
$$
\boxed{P(B\cup C)\leq2n^{k+1}(1-p)^{n-k}.}
$$
For $n\geq6$, $k=\lceil\sqrt n\rceil\leq n/2$ and $k^2\geq n$. Hence $P(A\cup B\cup C)<3n^{2k}(1-p)^{n/2}$. The logarithm of this bound is $\log3+2k\log n+(n/2)\log(1-p)$, which tends to $-\infty$ because $\sqrt n\log n=o(n)$. Thus the bad-event probability tends to zero.

The printed phrase “almost surely” must mean **asymptotically almost surely as $n\to\infty$**. At any fixed $n$, the empty graph has positive probability $(1-p)^{n^2}$, so the literal probability-one assertion is false.

When no bad event occurs, verify <Hall's marriage theorem>. If $0<|U|\leq k$, each vertex has more than $k$ neighbors because event $B$ is absent, so $|N(U)|\geq|U|$. If $k<|U|\leq n-k$, a failure would leave at least $k$ vertices outside $N(U)$, producing a forbidden empty $k$-square. If $|U|>n-k$, every vertex in $Y$ meets $U$ because event $C$ is absent; hence $N(U)=Y$. The empty set also satisfies Hall. Therefore the graph has a perfect matching whenever the three events are absent, proving the <random bipartite matching from empty-rectangle exclusion> result with probability tending to one.

= 18G
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Galois Theory}

= Solution
{parent=18G}

Modulo two the quartic has no root, and the only irreducible monic quadratic is $x^2+x+1$. Reducing the quartic modulo that quadratic gives remainder one. Hence the quartic is irreducible over $\mathbb F_2$. Its splitting field is $\mathbb F_{16}$, and $\boxed{\operatorname{Gal}_{\mathbb F_2}\cong C_4}$, generated by Frobenius.

Modulo three it factors as $(x-1)(x^3+x^2+x+2)$. The cubic has no root at zero, one or two and is irreducible, so its splitting field is $\mathbb F_{27}$ and $\boxed{\operatorname{Gal}_{\mathbb F_3}\cong C_3}$. Both reductions are square-free. Irreducibility modulo two makes the rational quartic irreducible, and its rational <Galois group> is transitive on four roots. The unramified factorization theorem supplies a four-cycle from the modulo-two reduction and a three-cycle from the modulo-three reduction. Its order is divisible by twelve and divides twenty-four. An order-twelve subgroup would have index two and equal $A_4$, which has no four-cycle. Thus $\boxed{\operatorname{Gal}_{\mathbb Q}\cong S_4}$.

= 19G
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Representation Theory}

= i
{parent=19g}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Every group element has a unique form $a^rb^s$, with $r\bmod7$ and $s\bmod3$. Conjugation by $b$ doubles the exponent of $a$, giving the two orbits $\{1,2,4\}$ and $\{3,6,5\}$. Conjugation by powers of $a$ runs through all seven elements of each nonidentity coset of $\langle a\rangle$. The classes are therefore
$$
\boxed{\{1\},\quad\{a,a^2,a^4\},\quad\{a^3,a^5,a^6\},\quad\{a^rb:0\leq r<7\},\quad\{a^rb^2:0\leq r<7\}.}
$$
Their sizes $1,3,3,7,7$ sum to twenty-one.

= ii
{parent=19g}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

In a one-dimensional <representation>, the images commute, so $ba=a^2b$ forces the image of $a$ to be one. With $\omega=e^{2\pi i/3}$ the three possibilities are
$$
\boxed{\rho_j(a)=1,\qquad\rho_j(b)=\omega^j,\qquad j=0,1,2.}
$$
They are inequivalent because they have distinct values at $b$.

= iii
{parent=19g}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

For a <character> $\chi$ of a <representation> of $H\leq K$, the <induced character> is
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Ind}_H^K\chi(g)=\frac1{|H|}\sum_{x\in K\,;\,x^{-1}gx\in H}\chi(x^{-1}gx).}
$$
The sum is zero when no conjugate of $g$ meets $H$.

= iv
{parent=19g}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

Put $\zeta=e^{2\pi i/7}$ and induce the <characters> $\chi_r(a)=\zeta^r$ of $H=\langle a\rangle$. Since $H$ is normal, the induced value outside $H$ is zero, while at $a^m$ it is $\zeta^{rm}+\zeta^{2rm}+\zeta^{4rm}$. The nonzero $r$ split into the two doubling orbits, so the two distinct induced <characters> have class values
$$
\boxed{(3,A,B,0,0),\qquad(3,B,A,0,0),}
$$
where $A=\zeta+\zeta^2+\zeta^4$ and $B=\zeta^3+\zeta^5+\zeta^6=\overline A$. Summing seventh roots gives $A+B=-1$, and multiplication gives $AB=2$, hence $|A|^2=|B|^2=2$. Their <character> <norms> are $(9+3\cdot2+3\cdot2)/21=1$, proving irreducibility. Together with the three degree-one <characters>, the sum of squared degrees is $3+2\cdot9=21$. Thus these are **all the remaining irreducible <characters>**.

= 20H
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Algebraic Topology}

= Solution
{parent=20H}

The absence of $n$-simplices implies absence of every higher-dimensional simplex, since any such simplex would have an $n$-face. Therefore $X$ has a CW structure of dimension at most $n-1$. Give $S^n$ its CW structure with one zero-cell and one $n$-cell. The <cellular approximation theorem> states that any map of CW complexes is homotopic to a cellular map, which sends each source skeleton into the corresponding target skeleton. For $n\geq1$, all target skeletons below dimension $n$ are the single zero-cell. The approximating map therefore sends all of $X$ there. Hence **every given map is null-homotopic**. This argument works for an infinite simplicial complex too; it does not require a finite subdivision or a finite collection of image simplices.

= 21F
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Linear Analysis}

= Solution
{parent=21F}

The <continuous dual space> $X^*$ consists of bounded complex-linear functionals $\phi:X\to\mathbb C$, with $\|\phi\|=\sup_{\|x\|\leq1}|\phi(x)|$. For $1\leq s<\infty$, $\ell^s$ consists of sequences with $\sum_j|x_j|^s<\infty$, <norm> $(\sum_j|x_j|^s)^{1/s}$; $\ell^\infty$ consists of bounded sequences with supremum <norm>.

For $a\in\ell^p$, define $\phi_a(x)=\sum_j a_jx_j$ on $\ell^q$. <Hölder's inequality> gives absolute convergence and $\|\phi_a\|\leq\|a\|_p$. For a finite truncation, choose $x_j=\overline{a_j}|a_j|^{p-2}/(\sum_{j\leq N}|a_j|^p)^{1/q}$ for $j\leq N$, zero elsewhere, interpreting the numerator as zero when $a_j=0$. Its $q$-norm is one and its pairing equals $(\sum_{j\leq N}|a_j|^p)^{1/p}$. Letting $N$ grow proves equality of the <norms>.

Conversely, for a bounded $\phi$ let $a_j=\phi(e_j)$. The same finite test vector gives $\sum_{j\leq N}|a_j|^p\leq\|\phi\|^p$, so $a\in\ell^p$. Finite truncations converge in $\ell^q$, and continuity gives $\phi(x)=\sum_j a_jx_j$. Thus $\boxed{(\ell^q)^*\cong\ell^p\text{ isometrically}.}$

For $\ell^1$, boundedness gives $|a_j|\leq\|\phi\|$, while any bounded sequence defines an absolutely convergent pairing bounded by $\|a\|_\infty\|x\|_1$. Density of finite sequences again proves surjectivity, and single-coordinate tests give <norm> equality. Hence $\boxed{(\ell^1)^*\cong\ell^\infty\text{ isometrically}.}$ The reversed argument fails for $\ell^\infty$: finite sequences are not dense in its supremum <norm>; their closure is $c_0$. For example the limit functional on convergent sequences extends by the <Hahn-Banach theorem> to $\ell^\infty$, vanishes on every $e_j$, yet takes value one on the constant-one sequence. It cannot be represented by pairing with an $\ell^1$ coefficient sequence.

= 22H
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Riemann Surfaces}

= Solution
{parent=22H}

A <meromorphic differential on a Riemann surface> has local expression $\omega=a(z)dz$ with a meromorphic coefficient. On overlapping coordinates it transforms by $a(z)dz=\widetilde a(w)dw$. For a <meromorphic function> $f$, the coefficients $f'(z)$ obey this transformation by the chain rule, so $df$ is such a differential. Two nonzero differentials have coefficient ratio $a/b$; the derivative factors cancel on overlaps, yielding a global <meromorphic function> $h$ with $\eta=h\omega$.

An invertible holomorphic coordinate change has a nonzero holomorphic derivative, so multiplying by its derivative changes no local zero or <pole> order. Thus the differential's divisor is well defined. For a compact connected surface, the canonical-degree theorem states $\sum_P\operatorname{ord}_P\omega=2g-2$, including negative orders for <poles>. Therefore $\boxed{g=1+\tfrac12\sum_P\operatorname{ord}_P\omega}$.

The projective closure has equation $U^2-V^2-W^2=0$. Its gradient $(2U,-2V,-2W)$ cannot vanish at a projective point, so it is nonsingular. At infinity $W=0$ gives the two points $[1:1:0]$ and $[1:-1:0]$. In the supplied sphere parameter,
$$
v(t)=\frac{2t}{t^2-1},\qquad dv=-\frac{2(t^2+1)}{(t^2-1)^2}\,dt.
$$
This has double <poles> at $t=\pm1$, the two infinity points, and simple zeros at $t=\pm i$. At the parameter infinity use $s=1/t$, giving $v=2s/(1-s^2)$ and $dv=2(1+s^2)(1-s^2)^{-2}ds$, which is regular and nonzero at $s=0$. Hence **$dv$ extends meromorphically over the whole projective curve**. Its order sum $2-4=-2$ gives genus zero, consistently with the stated biholomorphism from the sphere.

= 23H
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Differential Geometry}

= i
{parent=23h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For an oriented surface normal $N$ and a unit-speed curve tangent $T$, take signed <geodesic curvature> $k_g=(dT/ds)\cdot(T\times N)$. This convention uses boundary orientation with the region on the right; reversing to the usual left-hand boundary convention reverses both orientation and the corresponding curvature convention. With positive orientation consistent this way, the <Gauss-Bonnet theorem> for a region $D$ with piecewise smooth boundary is
$$
\boxed{\int_DK\,dA+\int_{\partial D}k_g\,ds+\sum\text{exterior turning angles}=2\pi\chi(D).}
$$
For a disk with smooth boundary there are no corner terms and $\chi(D)=1$. Stating the signed convention is necessary to reproduce the sign in part (ii).

= ii
{parent=23h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Let $T,n,B$ be the <Frenet frame> of the original space curve, with $T'=kn$, $n'=-kT+\tau B$, $B'=-\tau n$. Put $w=\sqrt{k^2+\tau^2}$. The normal-indicatrix arc-length satisfies $d\bar s/ds=w>0$, and its unit tangent is $\overline T=(-kT+\tau B)/w$. On the unit sphere its outward normal is $n$, and $\overline T\times n=(-kB-\tau T)/w$.

Differentiate $\overline T$ in $s$. The derivative of $1/w$ is parallel to $\overline T$ and contributes nothing to its projection onto $\overline T\times n$. The remaining derivative is $(-k'T+\tau'B-(k^2+\tau^2)n)/w$, giving
$$
k_g=\frac{\tau k'-k\tau'}{w^3}
=\boxed{-\frac{d}{ds}\arctan(\tau/k)\frac{ds}{d\bar s}.}
$$
With the alternative left-normal signed-curvature convention the displayed scalar changes sign, while the area conclusion is unchanged.

= iii
{parent=23h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Because $k>0$ and the original smooth closed curve has periodic Frenet data, $\arctan(\tau/k)$ is a continuous real-valued periodic function. Therefore $\int k_g\,d\bar s=-\oint d\arctan(\tau/k)=0$. The simple closed normal curve divides the sphere into two disks by the Jordan theorem. Apply <Gauss-Bonnet theorem> to either disk, choosing its consistent boundary orientation. Its <Gaussian curvature> is one and its total boundary curvature remains zero. Thus each region has area $2\pi$, proving **the two regions have equal area**.

= 24J
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Probability and Measure}

= a
{parent=24j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Working with measurable functions modulo equality almost everywhere, the sequence is Cauchy in <Lp space> if for every $\epsilon>0$ there exists $N$ such that $\|f_n-f_m\|_p<\epsilon$ for all $m,n\geq N$. This is a statement about integral <norm> distance, not pointwise convergence. Without quotienting null functions the displayed quantity is a seminorm, so the conventional $L^p$ normed space uses these equivalence classes.

= b
{parent=24j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Completeness means that every norm-Cauchy sequence has a limit belonging to the same <Lp space>, with $\|f_n-f\|_p\to0$. To prove it, choose a subsequence $f_{n_j}$ with $\|f_{n_{j+1}}-f_{n_j}\|_p\leq2^{-j}$. The functions
$$
H_N=|f_{n_1}|+\sum_{j=1}^N|f_{n_{j+1}}-f_{n_j}|
$$
have $p$-norm bounded by $\|f_{n_1}\|_p+1$, by <Minkowski inequality>. They increase to $H$, and <Fatou's lemma> gives $\int H^p\leq(\|f_{n_1}\|_p+1)^p$. Thus $H$ is finite almost everywhere, so the subsequence telescopes absolutely there to a measurable limit $f$ with $|f|\leq H$ and $f\in L^p$.

The same argument on the tails gives $\|f-f_{n_j}\|_p\leq\sum_{k\geq j}2^{-k}\to0$. Given $\epsilon$, choose such a subsequence term beyond the Cauchy threshold and use $\|f_n-f\|_p\leq\|f_n-f_{n_j}\|_p+\|f_{n_j}-f\|_p$. This proves convergence of the full sequence and **completeness for every $1\leq p<\infty$**, on an arbitrary measure space.

= i
{parent=24J}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For $m>n$, the $L^1$ difference is $\int_n^m x^{-1}dx=\log(m/n)$. Choosing $m=2n$ makes it $\log2$, so **the sequence is not Cauchy in $L^1$**. Its squared $L^2$ difference is $\int_n^m x^{-2}dx\leq1/n$, which tends uniformly to zero for $m>n$. Hence **it is Cauchy in $L^2$**, with <norm> limit $x^{-1}$ on $(1,\infty)$.

= ii
{parent=24J}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Here the $L^1$ difference is $\int_n^m x^{-2}dx\leq1/n$, and the squared $L^2$ difference is $\int_n^m x^{-4}dx\leq1/(3n^3)$. Both vanish uniformly for $m>n$, so **the sequence is Cauchy in both spaces**, with limit $x^{-2}$ in each.

= 25I
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Applied Probability}

= Solution
{parent=25I}

Let $m=\int t\,dF(t)$ be the finite mean service time. Poisson arrivals see the stationary occupancy distribution, so the accepted rate is $\lambda(1-\pi)$. An accepted arrival's independent service time still has mean $m$. <Little's law> applied to occupied servers gives $\boxed{L=\lambda(1-\pi)m}$; it does not require exponential service times.

For the cafe, $\lambda=3/20$ and the mean table-occupation time is $(2/3)20+(1/3)30=70/3$. Thus $L=(7/2)(1-\pi)$ and the long-run revenue rate, at $2/5$ per occupied table per unit time, is
$$
\boxed{R=\frac75(1-\pi).}
$$
Couple the cafe to an infinite-table system using the same arrivals and assigned service durations. Each accepted finite-cafe party is also present in the infinite system for exactly the same interval, so finite occupancy never exceeds infinite occupancy. Singles and pairs form independent thinned Poisson arrival streams of rates $1/10$ and $1/20$. Their infinite-server occupancies are independent Poisson variables of means two and $3/2$, hence their total is $N\sim\operatorname{Poisson}(7/2)$. Consequently $\boxed{\pi\leq P(N\geq23)}$.

The exponential Markov bound $P(N\geq r)\leq\exp[\mu(e^t-1)-tr]$ is minimized at $t=\log(r/\mu)$. With $\mu=7/2,r=23$ it is $e^{-7/2}(7e/46)^{23}<5\times10^{-11}$. This is far below $10^{-3}$, so blocking and the associated revenue loss are negligible in the specified model.

= 26I
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Principles of Statistics}

= Solution
{parent=26I}

A <loss function> $L(\theta,a)$ measures the cost of taking action $a$ at parameter $\theta$. A <decision rule> $\delta$ selects an action from the data; its <risk function> is $R(\theta,\delta)=E_\theta L(\theta,\delta(X))$. A <Bayes estimator> minimizes the prior-averaged risk, or equivalently minimizes posterior expected loss at almost every observation. An admissible rule has no competing rule of no greater risk everywhere and strictly smaller risk somewhere. To construct a Bayes rule, form the posterior and minimize its expected loss over the allowed actions. Under squared error and finite posterior second moments the unique action is the posterior mean.

Put $S=|X|^2$. The given fourth moment and independence give $E_vS=nv$, $E_vS^2=n(n+2)v^2$. Thus
$$
R(v,\alpha S)=v^2[n(n+2)\alpha^2-2n\alpha+1]
=v^2\left[n(n+2)\left(\alpha-\frac1{n+2}\right)^2+\frac2{n+2}\right].
$$
Every $\alpha\ne1/(n+2)$ is strictly dominated at every $v>0$ by that coefficient. Hence **it cannot be a finite-risk Bayes rule for any proper prior**.

To make the second requested comparison, let $m_j=E_\pi v^j$ and assume finite quadratic <Bayes risk>. For the affine rules the integrated risk is
$$
r(\alpha,\beta)=[n(n+2)\alpha^2-2n\alpha+1]m_2+2\beta(n\alpha-1)m_1+\beta^2.
$$
With $\alpha$ fixed, the minimum occurs at $\beta=(1-n\alpha)m_1$. Since $m_1>0$, this strictly improves the homogeneous rule unless $\alpha=1/n$, reducing risk by $(1-n\alpha)^2m_1^2$. Therefore **a homogeneous rule with $\alpha\ne1/n$ is not Bayes**, as requested. Combining the two restrictions excludes every homogeneous coefficient.

For a proper prior without global finite moments, the same nonvacuous conclusion follows from the posterior-mean condition. Let
$$
m(s)=\int_0^\infty v^{-n/2}e^{-s/(2v)}f_0(v)dv,\qquad
M_1(s)=\int_0^\infty v^{1-n/2}e^{-s/(2v)}f_0(v)dv.
$$
For $s>0$, $M_1'(s)=-m(s)/2$. If the posterior mean were $\alpha s$, positivity requires $\alpha>0$ and $M_1=\alpha s m$. Differentiation forces $m(s)=Cs^{-1-1/(2\alpha)}$. But the marginal density of $S$ would then be a constant times $s^{n/2-2-1/(2\alpha)}$, a pure power not integrable at both zero and infinity. This contradicts a proper prior's normalized predictive distribution. This is the <proper-prior obstruction to homogeneous variance Bayes rules>.

The finite-loss qualification matters: allowing all posterior losses to equal infinity makes every action a vacuous minimizer. For example, with $n=1$ and proper density $f_0(v)=(1+v)^{-2}$, the posterior second moment diverges at positive observations, so a literal infinite-loss definition would defeat the printed assertion. Ordinary meaningful Bayes rules exclude that convention.

= 27J
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Stochastic Financial Models}

= a
{parent=27j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

At the node with stock price thirty, both possible next prices exceed thirty-three, the value of thirty invested at gross rate $11/10$. Take no position at time zero. If the first move is good, borrow thirty at time one and buy one share; if it is bad, do nothing. At time two sell the share for forty-five or thirty-six and repay thirty-three, leaving twelve or three. The strategy has zero initial cost, no possible loss, and positive profit on the good first move, so it is an <arbitrage>.

= b
{parent=27j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

At gross rate $R=5/4$, the conditional up probabilities of the <equivalent martingale measure> are
$$
\boxed{q_0=\frac{(5/4)15-12}{30-12}=\frac38,\quad
q_u=\frac{(5/4)30-36}{45-36}=\frac16,\quad
q_d=\frac{(5/4)12-10}{16-10}=\frac56.}
$$
All lie strictly between zero and one. The four terminal path probabilities are $1/16,5/16,25/48,5/48$ in the order good-good, good-bad, bad-good, bad-bad.

= c
{parent=27j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The terminal <European put option> payoffs are zero at forty-five, thirty-six and sixteen, and five at ten. At the good first node its value is zero. At the bad node its continuation value is $(4/5)[(5/6)0+(1/6)5]=2/3$, below the exercise payoff three, so optimal exercise occurs there. At time zero the continuation value is $(4/5)[(3/8)0+(5/8)3]=\boxed{3/2}$, exceeding the immediate payoff zero.

To hedge one sold <American put option>, short $1/6$ of a share and put four in the bank at time zero. The portfolio costs $-15/6+4=3/2$, exactly the premium. At time one the bank holds five. After a good move the short position costs five to close, exhausting the portfolio and liability. After a bad move it costs two to close, leaving three for the exercised put. Thus after two bad periods the rational buyer already exercised at time one, and **the hedge pays three and closes then; there is no remaining time-two obligation**. Physical delivery gives the same accounting by immediately selling the stock received on exercise.

= d
{parent=27j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

If the first move is bad but the buyer misses exercise, the remaining European claim is worth $2/3$, whereas the existing hedge is worth three. At that node rehedge the continuation claim: its stock holding is $(0-5)/(16-10)=-5/6$ and its bank holding is $2/3+(5/6)12=32/3$.

Starting from the original short $1/6$ and bank balance five, short another $2/3$ share for eight, giving bank cash thirteen. Retain $32/3$ for the new hedge and withdraw $\boxed{7/3}$ as guaranteed time-one profit. At time two the retained bank grows to $40/3$. If price is sixteen, covering the short costs $40/3$ and the put expires worthless. If price is ten, covering costs $25/3$ and the remaining five pays the put. Thus the profit does not depend on the second move. Investing the withdrawn profit at the riskless rate makes it $35/12$ at time two.

= 28I
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Optimization and Control}

= Solution
{parent=28I}

Interpret unit-intensity Gaussian white noise as $dW_t/dt$. Differentiating $z=x+(T-t)y$ cancels the two $y\,dt$ terms and gives
$$
\boxed{dz=(T-t)u\,dt+(T-t)dW_t,\qquad z(T)=x(T).}
$$
The cost is terminal $z(T)^2$ plus the same running $u^2$. Write $h=T-t$ and guess the value $V(t,z)=P(t)z^2+C(t)$. The <Hamilton-Jacobi-Bellman equation> is
$$
V_t+\tfrac12h^2V_{zz}+\min_u(u^2+huV_z)=0,\qquad V(T,z)=z^2.
$$
Completing the square gives $u_*=-hPz$. Equating powers yields $P'=h^2P^2$, $C'=-h^2P$, with $P(T)=1,C(T)=0$. Integration gives
$$
\boxed{P(t)=\frac1{1+(T-t)^3/3},\quad C(t)=\log[1+(T-t)^3/3],\quad
u_*=-\frac{(T-t)z}{1+(T-t)^3/3}.}
$$
Indeed $V_t+\mathcal L^uV+u^2=(u+hPz)^2\geq0$, and the Itô verification identity attains equality at this control. This proves optimality for admissible controls of finite expected quadratic cost. The additive noise changes only $C$, not the feedback gain, so **this is certainty-equivalence control**. A different constant noise intensity multiplies $C$ but leaves the control unchanged, as in the <terminal-position reduction of double-integrator control>.

= 29C
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Partial Differential Equations}

= Solution
{parent=29C}

For positive time, convolution with the <heat kernel> gives
$$
w(t,x)=(K_t*g)(x),\qquad K_t(x)=(4\pi t)^{-n/2}e^{-|x|^2/(4t)}.
$$
Differentiation under the integral verifies the <heat equation>, and the <approximate identity> property gives the initial value. Taking absolute values under the integral and using $e^{-|x-y|^2/(4t)}\leq1$ gives
$$
\boxed{|w(t,x)|\leq(4\pi t)^{-n/2}\|g\|_1.}
$$
The printed inequality omits the modulus even though $g$ is complex-valued. A complex number has no compatible order, so the displayed absolute-value estimate is the meaningful correction.

Substitution of $u=e^{it}v$ reduces the forced <partial differential equation> to $(i-\Delta)v=f$. With the <Fourier transform> convention for which $\widehat{\Delta v}=-|\xi|^2\widehat v$, set
$$
\widehat v(\xi)=\frac{\widehat f(\xi)}{i+|\xi|^2}.
$$
The denominator never vanishes for real $\xi$. Every derivative of its reciprocal has at most <polynomial> growth, so multiplying a <Schwartz function> by it gives another <Schwartz function>. The inverse <Fourier transform> therefore supplies the required $v$.

For the prescribed initial value the full solution is $u(t,x)=e^{it}v(x)+K_t*(g-v)(x)$. Applying the <heat kernel> estimate to the <Schwartz function> $g-v$ proves the stronger uniform conclusion
$$
\boxed{\sup_x|u(t,x)-e^{it}v(x)|\leq(4\pi t)^{-n/2}\|g-v\|_1\longrightarrow0.}
$$

= 30A
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Asymptotic Methods}

= Solution
{parent=30A}

<Watson lemma> expands a <Laplace integral> by integrating the small-$t$ expansion term by term. More precisely, if $f(t)\sim\sum_{j\geq0}a_jt^{\lambda+j-1}$ with $\operatorname{Re}\lambda>0$, and the tail satisfies bounds permitting this localization, then
$$
\int_0^\infty e^{-xt}f(t)\,dt\sim\sum_{j\geq0}a_j\Gamma(\lambda+j)x^{-\lambda-j}.
$$
For ordinary powers $f(t)\sim\sum c_jt^j$, the coefficients are $c_jj!$. For the Gaussian weight, put $s=t^2$ separately on the positive and negative half-lines. The integral becomes a <Laplace integral> with amplitude $[f(\sqrt s)+f(-\sqrt s)]/(2\sqrt s)$. Odd terms cancel and <Watson lemma> gives
$$
\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}e^{-xt^2}f(t)\,dt\sim\sum_{j\geq0}c_{2j}\Gamma(j+\tfrac12)x^{-j-1/2}.
$$
These assertions concern asymptotic expansions, not necessarily convergent series.

For the complex integral in the lower half-plane, the identity $(z-t)^{-1}=i\int_0^\infty e^{-i(z-t)s}\,ds$ and the Gaussian <Fourier transform> give
$$
I(z)=i\sqrt\pi\int_0^\infty e^{-s^2/4-izs}\,ds
=\boxed{i\pi e^{-z^2}\operatorname{erfc}(iz)}.
$$
The last expression, initially obtained by completing the square, is entire and therefore gives the requested <analytic continuation>. Expanding the denominator formally, with odd Gaussian moments zero, gives the algebraic expansion
$$
\mathcal A(z)=\frac{\sqrt\pi}{z}\sum_{j\geq0}\frac{(2j-1)!!}{(2z^2)^j}
=\frac{\sqrt\pi}{z}\left(1+\frac1{2z^2}+\frac3{4z^4}+\cdots\right).
$$
Here $(-1)!!=1$. The <complementary error function> expansion shows $I(z)\sim\mathcal A(z)$ on closed subsectors of $-5\pi/4<\arg z<\pi/4$, using an unwrapped argument centered on the lower half-plane.

In the upper half-plane, the directly evaluated real-contour integral is $J(z)=-i\pi e^{-z^2}\operatorname{erfc}(-iz)$. Since $\operatorname{erfc}(w)+\operatorname{erfc}(-w)=2$, continuation from below gives
$$
\boxed{I(z)=J(z)+2\pi i e^{-z^2},\qquad I(z)\sim\mathcal A(z)+2\pi i e^{-z^2}.}
$$
The upper expansion for $J$ holds on closed subsectors of $-\pi/4<\arg z<5\pi/4$. The added term is the <residue> acquired by the continued contour passing the <pole>; it is exponentially dominant in the upper sector $\pi/4<\arg z<3\pi/4$ and exponentially small near either real ray. On the real axis the continued value is the principal-value integral plus $i\pi e^{-x^2}$.

To make the <Stokes phenomenon> convention explicit, the exponential switches its asymptotic multiplier across the real rays $\arg z=0,\pi$; these are the Stokes switching lines. The diagonal rays $\arg z=\pi/4+j\pi/2$ separate exponential growth from decay and are often called anti-Stokes lines, or critical boundaries when regions are classified by dominance. Thus both sets of critical directions and the exact jump are specified, independently of which terminology is used. This is the <Gaussian Cauchy transform and its Stokes jump>.

= 31A
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Integrable Systems}

= a
{parent=31a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

There is a literal inconsistency in the stated <Riemann-Hilbert problem>: a jump <matrix> $S(k)$ tending to zero is incompatible with $\mu_\pm\to I$. Along the real axis the conjugating exponentials are unitary, so the right side of the jump tends to zero while the left side tends to $I$. **No normalized solution satisfies the printed decay condition.** The intended condition is $S(k)\to I$, or replacement of $S$ in the jump by $I+S$ when $S$ denotes its decaying part.

For this corrected problem assume a regular, invertible, pole-free solution, differentiable in $x,t$, with the required expansion uniformly at infinity. These conditions matter: arbitrary jump data need not be solvable, and <poles> would contribute extra terms. Write $\sigma=\sigma_3$ and $\mu=I+\mu_1/k+\mu_2/k^2+\cdots$. Matching the nondecaying coefficients in the two differential expressions yields
$$
\boxed{Q=i[\sigma,\mu_1],\qquad A=2i[\sigma,\mu_1]=2Q,\qquad
B=2i[\sigma,\mu_2]-A\mu_1.}
$$
The first expression makes $Q$ off-diagonal.

These matches really establish the differential equations, rather than just formal identities. Let $G=e^{-i(kx+2k^2t)\sigma}S(k)e^{i(kx+2k^2t)\sigma}$. It satisfies $G_x=-ik[\sigma,G]$ and $G_t=-2ik^2[\sigma,G]$. Differentiating $\mu_+=\mu_-G$ consequently shows that
$$
R_x=(\mu_x+ik[\sigma,\mu]-Q\mu)\mu^{-1},\qquad
R_t=(\mu_t+2ik^2[\sigma,\mu]-(kA+B)\mu)\mu^{-1}
$$
have matching boundary values. They therefore extend holomorphically across the contour. The chosen coefficients make both residuals $O(k^{-1})$ at infinity. Their absence of <poles> and <Liouville theorem> imply $R_x=R_t=0$, proving the required <Lax pair> under the corrected hypotheses. This is <matrix NLS reconstruction from a normalized Riemann-Hilbert problem>.

= b
{parent=31a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The $k^{-1}$ coefficient of the first equation is $\mu_{1x}+i[\sigma,\mu_2]=Q\mu_1$. Substitution in the expression for $B$ gives $B=-2\mu_{1x}$. Decompose $\mu_1$ into diagonal and off-diagonal parts. Since $Q=i[\sigma,\mu_1]$, its off-diagonal part is $iQ\sigma/2$. Thus
$$
(\mu_{1x})_{\mathrm{off}}=\tfrac i2Q_x\sigma,\qquad
(\mu_{1x})_{\mathrm{diag}}=(Q\mu_1)_{\mathrm{diag}}=\tfrac i2Q^2\sigma.
$$
The commutator in the $k^{-1}$ equation has zero diagonal; multiplication of two off-diagonal two-by-two <matrices> is diagonal. Combining the two parts proves
$$
\boxed{A=2Q,\qquad B=-i(Q^2+Q_x)\sigma_3.}
$$

= c
{parent=31a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Put $\Psi=\mu e^{-i(kx+2k^2t)\sigma}$. The <Lax pair> becomes $\Psi_x=U\Psi$, $\Psi_t=V\Psi$, with
$$
U=-ik\sigma+Q,\qquad V=-2ik^2\sigma+2kQ-i(Q^2+Q_x)\sigma.
$$
Equality of mixed derivatives, using invertibility, gives the <zero-curvature condition> $U_t-V_x+[U,V]=0$. The terms quadratic in $k$ cancel. The terms linear in $k$ cancel because $\sigma Q=-Q\sigma$, $\sigma Q_x=-Q_x\sigma$ and $Q^2$ commutes with $\sigma$. For the constant term, the identities
$$
[Q,Q^2\sigma]=2Q^3\sigma,\qquad
[Q,Q_x\sigma]=(QQ_x+Q_xQ)\sigma
$$
cancel the differentiated $Q^2$ term, leaving $Q_t+iQ_{xx}\sigma-2iQ^3\sigma=0$. Multiplying by $i$ proves
$$
\boxed{iQ_t-Q_{xx}\sigma_3+2Q^3\sigma_3=0.}
$$
Once $S$ is known, its time dependence enters through the explicit exponential conjugation. The boundary relation and normalization form a linear problem for $\mu$, and $Q=i[\sigma,\mu_1]$ reconstructs the nonlinear solution. This is the requested linearization. Its disadvantage as an initial-value method is that it does not itself determine $S$ from a prescribed $Q(x,0)$. The <inverse scattering transform> supplies that direct-scattering step, including any discrete spectral data and their <pole> conditions, before reconstruction. Solvability cannot be inferred merely from writing an arbitrary jump.

= 32D
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Principles of Quantum Mechanics}

= Solution
{parent=32D}

Addition of two spin-one <angular momenta> gives $J=0,1,2$, with $M=-J,-J+1,\ldots,J$. To obtain the $J=1$ multiplet, its highest-weight state must have $M=1$ and be killed by $J_+=J_+^{(1)}+J_+^{(2)}$. On the two possible product states, the raising operator gives the same multiple of $|1;1\rangle$, so their difference is required. Normalization and successive lowering give
$$
\boxed{\begin{aligned}
|1,1\rangle&=\frac{|1;0\rangle-|0;1\rangle}{\sqrt2},\\
|1,0\rangle&=\frac{|1;-1\rangle-|-1;1\rangle}{\sqrt2},\\
|1,-1\rangle&=\frac{|0;-1\rangle-|-1;0\rangle}{\sqrt2}.
\end{aligned}}
$$
For example $J_-|1,1\rangle=|1;-1\rangle-|-1;1\rangle=\sqrt2|1,0\rangle$, and lowering once more gives the last expression. These states are all antisymmetric under interchanging the two constituents.

Identical spin-one particles are <bosons>, so the total state must be symmetric. Its $J=1$ spin factor is antisymmetric and must therefore multiply an antisymmetric spatial factor. Two particles in the same one-particle orbital have zero antisymmetric spatial state. The lowest allowed choice is
$$
\frac{\psi_1(r_A)\psi_2(r_B)-\psi_2(r_A)\psi_1(r_B)}{\sqrt2}\,|1,M\rangle,
\qquad \boxed{E=E_1+E_2},\quad M=-1,0,1.
$$
For distinguishable particles there is no exchange-symmetry constraint. Both can occupy the ground orbital, giving $\psi_1(r_A)\psi_1(r_B)|1,M\rangle$ and **lowest energy $\boxed{2E_1}$**, again with three spin states.

= 33B
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Applications of Quantum Mechanics}

= Solution
{parent=33B}

A <Bravais lattice> is the set of all integer combinations of linearly independent primitive translation vectors. Its <reciprocal lattice> consists of $g$ satisfying $g\cdot l\in2\pi\mathbb Z$ for every translation $l$; equivalently $e^{ig\cdot l}=1$.

The original PDF's third family has a bare $j$, not $n_2j$. Taken literally, the listed set is not the claimed <Bravais lattice>: the claimed primitive vector $a(i+k)/2$ does not occur. Its half-integer first and third coordinates force it into that third family, whose second coordinate is always $a$, not zero. The intended repair is **$j\mapsto n_2j$ in the third family**. The following conclusions apply to that corrected set.

The four corrected families are exactly
$$
L=\frac a2\{(h,k,l)\in\mathbb Z^3:h+k+l\text{ is even}\}.
$$
The allowed parity patterns are all even, or exactly two odd coordinates. An integer combination of the proposed primitive vectors has coordinates $(a/2)(m_2+m_3,m_1+m_3,m_1+m_2)$, so lies in this set. Conversely, solve
$$
m_1=\tfrac12(k+l-h),\quad m_2=\tfrac12(h+l-k),\quad m_3=\tfrac12(h+k-l).
$$
All three are integers precisely when $h+k+l$ is even. This proves that the corrected set is the <face-centered cubic lattice> with the proposed primitive basis.

Direct dot products give $a_i\cdot b_j=2\pi\delta_{ij}$ for
$$
\boxed{b_1=\frac{2\pi}a(-1,1,1),\quad b_2=\frac{2\pi}a(1,-1,1),\quad b_3=\frac{2\pi}a(1,1,-1).}
$$
These vectors generate the entire <reciprocal lattice>, since the coefficients of an arbitrary reciprocal vector in this basis are its integer pairings with the $a_i$. Its vectors are $(2\pi/a)(h,k,l)$ with all three coordinates of the same parity, the <body-centered cubic lattice>.

In <Bragg scattering>, translating a scattering atom by $l$ changes the amplitude by $e^{i(k-k')\cdot l}$. Constructive interference from every lattice translate requires this factor to be one, so $k'-k=g$ is a <reciprocal lattice> vector. For elastic scattering, $|k'|=|k|$; the chord between these two vectors has length $2|k|\sin(\theta/2)$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\sin(\theta/2)=\frac{|g|}{2|k|}.}
$$
The shortest nonzero reciprocal vectors are the $(111)$ family of length $(2\pi/a)\sqrt3$, followed by the $(200)$ family of length $(2\pi/a)2$. Identical atoms on this <Bravais lattice> introduce no additional basis extinction. If both families are accessible at the incident wavelength, their scattering angles obey **$\boxed{\sin(\theta_1/2)/\sin(\theta_2/2)=\sqrt3/2}$**.

= 34D
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Statistical Physics}

= Solution
{parent=34D}

Periodic boundary conditions give wavevectors $(2\pi/L)(n_x,n_y)$. In the continuum, large-box limit each state occupies area $(2\pi/L)^2$ in wavevector space. A circular annulus therefore contains $[L^2/(2\pi)^2]2\pi k\,dk$ spinless states. Since $\epsilon=\hbar^2k^2/(2m)$, the <density of states> is
$$
\boxed{g_0=\frac{mL^2}{2\pi\hbar^2}.}
$$
At finite $L$ the exact spectrum is discrete; this formula is its continuum density.

With electron spin the two Zeeman branches are $\epsilon_{\mathrm{kin}}\mp\mu H$. The requested calculation includes this spin coupling and neglects orbital coupling to the field; for a perpendicular field, <Landau levels> would change the result. It is appropriate for a field parallel to the <ideal> two-dimensional plane, or for the stated spin-only model. Taking $\mu H\geq0$, the lower branch starts at $-\mu H$, and the upper one at $\mu H$, so
$$
\boxed{g(\epsilon)=\begin{cases}0,&\epsilon<-\mu H,\\g_0,&-\mu H<\epsilon<\mu H,\\2g_0,&\epsilon>\mu H.\end{cases}}
$$
Construct the ground state by filling the lowest one-electron levels subject to the <Pauli exclusion principle>. If the <Fermi energy> $\epsilon_F$ exceeds $\mu H$, the two populations are $N_+=g_0(\epsilon_F+\mu H)$ and $N_-=g_0(\epsilon_F-\mu H)$. Consequently
$$
\boxed{N=2g_0\epsilon_F=\frac{mL^2}{\pi\hbar^2}\epsilon_F,\qquad
M=\mu(N_+-N_-)=\frac{\mu^2mL^2}{\pi\hbar^2}H.}
$$
The <ground state energy>, including the Zeeman term, follows by integrating the energy over each occupied branch:
$$
E_0=g_0\int_{-\mu H}^{\epsilon_F}\epsilon\,d\epsilon+
 g_0\int_{\mu H}^{\epsilon_F}\epsilon\,d\epsilon
=g_0(\epsilon_F^2-\mu^2H^2)
=\boxed{\frac{\pi\hbar^2N^2}{2mL^2}-\frac12MH.}
$$

= 35B
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Electrodynamics}

= Solution
{parent=35B}

Choose polar angle $\theta$ from the acceleration direction. Then $|n\times q\dot v|^2=q^2|\dot v|^2\sin^2\theta$. Integrating over the <solid angle> gives $2\pi\int_0^\pi\sin^3\theta\,d\theta=8\pi/3$, and hence the specified normalization gives <Larmor formula>
$$
\boxed{P=\frac{\mu_0q^2}{6\pi}|\dot v|^2.}
$$
This uses the source's convention for radiated power; standard SI normalization has an additional factor $1/c$ in both given power formulas.

To leading order when $W\ll E$, neglect the loss while computing the head-on trajectory. Mechanical energy conservation gives $\tfrac12m\dot r^2+V(r)=E=V(r_0)$ and acceleration magnitude $|V'(r)|/m$. On each leg,
$$
dt=\sqrt{\frac m2}\frac{|dr|}{\sqrt{V(r_0)-V(r)}}.
$$
The incoming and outgoing legs contribute equally. Multiplying the power by this time element, adding both legs, and assuming the integral converges gives
$$
\boxed{W\simeq\frac{\mu_0q^2}{3\pi m^2}\sqrt{\frac m2}
\int_{r_0}^\infty\frac{[V'(r)]^2}{\sqrt{V(r_0)-V(r)}}\,dr.}
$$
Using the unperturbed turning point is justified at this order in the small radiative loss, for a regular turning trajectory.

= 36E
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Fluid Dynamics II}

= Solution
{parent=36E}

The incompressible <Navier-Stokes equations> are $u_t+(u\cdot\nabla)u=-\nabla p/\rho+\nu\Delta u$ and $\nabla\cdot u=0$, with no penetration and no slip at a stationary rigid wall. The <Euler limit> takes $\nu\to0$ at fixed distance from the wall, leaving the inviscid <Euler equations> and a no-penetration condition; imposing tangential no slip on that outer problem generally overdetermines it. The <Prandtl limit> resolves a shrinking wall layer in which transverse viscous diffusion remains comparable to convection and restores no slip.

For the steady flat-plate <boundary layer>, $\delta/x\ll1$, continuity makes $v/U=O(\delta/x)$, and $u_{xx}$ is small compared with $u_{yy}$. Normal momentum makes pressure independent of $y$ to leading order. The constant outer speed makes the outer pressure gradient, and hence $p_x$ throughout the layer, zero. The leading equations are $uu_x+vu_y=\nu u_{yy}$ and $u_x+v_y=0$. With the <stream function> convention $u=\psi_y$, $v=-\psi_x$, these give
$$
\psi_y\psi_{xy}-\psi_x\psi_{yy}=\nu\psi_{yyy}.
$$
Balancing $U^2/x$ against $\nu U/\delta^2$ gives $\delta=\sqrt{\nu x/U}$. There is no independent streamwise scale for the semi-infinite plate, motivating the similarity solution
$$
\boxed{\eta=y\sqrt{\frac U{\nu x}},\qquad \psi=\sqrt{\nu Ux}\,f(\eta).}
$$
It gives $u=Uf'$ and $v=\tfrac12\sqrt{\nu U/x}(\eta f'-f)$. Substitution yields $uu_x+vu_y=-U^2ff''/(2x)$ and $\nu u_{yy}=U^2f'''/x$, so the <Blasius equation> and conditions are
$$
\boxed{f'''+\tfrac12ff''=0,\qquad f(0)=f'(0)=0,\qquad f'(\infty)=1.}
$$
The first two conditions impose no penetration and no slip; the last matches the outer stream.

The wall shear on one face is $\tau(x)=\rho\nu u_y(x,0)=\rho U\sqrt{\nu U/x}\,f''(0)$. Integration from zero to $L$ gives $2\rho U^2Lf''(0)/\sqrt{UL/\nu}$ on one face. With the same flow on both faces, the total <drag force> per unit width is
$$
\boxed{F=\frac{4\rho U^2L}{\sqrt{UL/\nu}}\frac{f''(0)}{[f'(\infty)]^2}.}
$$
Our normalization has $f'(\infty)=1$, so the displayed denominator is one. The factor four refers to both faces; for a plate wetted on only one face the drag is half this value.

= 37E
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Waves}

= Solution
{parent=37E}

Substitution of $e^{i(kx-\omega t)}$ gives the <dispersion relation> $\boxed{\omega(k)=Uk+k^3}$. The <phase velocity> is $U+k^2$, whereas the <group velocity> is $U+3k^2$. Thus nonzero-wavenumber crests move more slowly than their wave packet; the velocities coincide at zero wavenumber.

Fourier evolution gives $\phi(Vt,t)=\int A(k)e^{it\Phi(k)}\,dk$, where $\Phi(k)=(V-U)k-k^3$. For $V>U$ the two nondegenerate stationary points are $k=\pm\kappa$, $\kappa=\sqrt{(V-U)/3}$. At these points $\Phi(\pm\kappa)=\pm2\kappa^3$ and $\Phi''(\pm\kappa)=\mp6\kappa$. Expanding the phase quadratically near each point and evaluating the oscillatory Gaussian integral gives the <stationary phase method>
$$
\boxed{\phi(Vt,t)\sim\sqrt{\frac{\pi}{3\kappa t}}\left[
A(\kappa)e^{i(2\kappa^3t-\pi/4)}+A(-\kappa)e^{-i(2\kappa^3t-\pi/4)}\right].}
$$
For a real initial field, $A(-\kappa)=\overline{A(\kappa)}$, so the two terms are conjugate. The approximation assumes a sufficiently smooth decaying amplitude; if a leading coefficient vanishes, the next stationary-phase terms determine that contribution.

For $V<U$, $\Phi'(k)=V-U-3k^2<0$ everywhere. There are no real stationary points, so no leading propagating packet reaches such a ray. For a Schwartz amplitude, repeated integration by parts with $e^{it\Phi}=(it\Phi')^{-1}\partial_ke^{it\Phi}$ makes the contribution $O(t^{-N})$ for every fixed $N$. With weaker data the decay rate depends on their regularity. This agrees with the minimum <group velocity> being $U$.

= 38A
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Numerical Analysis}

= a
{parent=38a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Use the stage solution continuous from $k_2=f(y)$ as $h\to0$. Its displacement is $\delta=h[(1-a)f+ak_2]=hf+ah^2f'f+O(h^3)$. Taylor expansion of $f(y+\delta)$ gives
$$
k_2=f+hf'f+h^2\left(af'^2f+\tfrac12f''f^2\right)+O(h^3).
$$
Consequently the <Runge-Kutta method> advances by
$$
y_{n+1}=y+hf+\tfrac12h^2f'f+h^3\left(\tfrac a2f'^2f+\tfrac14f''f^2\right)+O(h^4).
$$
The exact solution has third-order term $h^3(f'^2f+f''f^2)/6$. The first two orders agree for every real $a$, but the $f''f^2$ coefficient is always $1/4$ instead of $1/6$. Therefore **the general nonlinear method has $\boxed{\text{order }2}$ for every fixed real $a$**. The special value $a=1/3$ matches the linear test equation at third order but does not raise the nonlinear order. This is the <two-stage Runge-Kutta family with an implicit second stage>.

= b
{parent=38a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For the linear test equation $f(y)=\lambda y$ put $z=h\lambda$. The second stage solves $(1-az)k_2=\lambda y[1+(1-a)z]$, and substitution into the update gives the <stability function>
$$
\boxed{R(z)=\frac{1+(1-a)z+(1/2-a)z^2}{1-az}.}
$$
<A-stability> requires no <poles> and $|R(z)|\leq1$ throughout the closed left half-plane. If $a\ne1/2$, the quadratic numerator gives unbounded magnitude as $z\to-\infty$; when $a=0$ the denominator is constant and the same conclusion holds. Thus $a=1/2$ is necessary. At this value $R(z)=(1+z/2)/(1-z/2)$, whose only <pole> is at positive $z=2$. Moreover
$$
|1-z/2|^2-|1+z/2|^2=-2\operatorname{Re}z\geq0
$$
on the left half-plane. Hence **the method is A-stable exactly for $\boxed{a=1/2}$**. Its limit at negative infinity is $-1$, so it is not <L-stable>.
